<BaseLayout title="Fruit Hunting — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Fruit we went looking for, 2022–2026: three Hawaiian islands, white strawberries in Portland, a strawberry stand in Irvine, a pot of strawberries at home, and durian, cherimoya and sugar apples from San Gabriel to Sanya.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022 – 2026</p>
  <h1>Fruit Hunting</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Tropical fruit from three Hawaiʻi trips, strawberries from every kind of source, and a few durians.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 years · July 2022 – September 2026</strong><br />
    three Hawaiian trips · two fruit hauls, one on a bench and one on a table · a box of coffee cherries and a bunch of
    green bananas · white strawberries in Portland, Maine · a farm stand in Irvine · a strawberry plant
    at home · an Asian supermarket in San Gabriel · four durians, a table of cherimoya and three sugar apples
  </div>

  <p>
    This post collects the fruit. It turns up in a lot of our photos: from markets and farms on trips, and from
    a farm stand, the supermarket and the Asian supermarket at home. Most of these moments already appear in the trip posts, one photo at a time. Here they are
    together, with a few that never made it into any post.
  </p>
  <p>
    The photos come from both of our phones. My wife's camera roll has most of the strawberries; mine has most of the
    durian. Where she found something on her own, I say so; "we" means we were both there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hawaii">Hawaiʻi, three times</h2>
  <p>
    Hawaiʻi is the easiest place to go fruit hunting that we have been to. The fruit turns up at markets, on
    tables and benches, and on the trees along the way.
  </p>

  <h3>Hilo Farmers Market, September 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The best fruit stop in Hawaiʻi is the <strong>Hilo Farmers Market</strong>, and we have been twice. Hilo is on
    the wet, windward side of the Big Island, where much of the island's fruit is grown, and the market is a long
    covered hall on the corner of downtown, with a mural of sliced fruit along the back wall and stalls under blue
    umbrellas.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first time was mine, without my wife. On September 12, 2025, I drove across the island and reached the market
    at half past twelve. There were piles of papaya, bananas, pineapples, avocados, dragon fruit and rambutan, the red,
    hairy relative of the lychee. I bought far more than two days could use. Lunch, a few blocks away, came with a
    drink served in a hollowed-out pineapple. The full day is in
    <a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Fishponds, a Crater and the Milky Way</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-market.webp'} alt="The covered Hilo Farmers Market: rows of fruit and vegetable stalls under blue umbrellas, with a colorful mural of sliced fruit along the back wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hilo Farmers Market, 12:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-stall.webp'} alt="A market stall with a tray of papayas, a tray of yellow bananas, pineapples, avocados and green bananas on tables draped in green cloth." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Papayas, bananas and pineapples.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The market also had durian, and it was the best of the haul. Back on the Kohala Coast at the
    <strong>Mauna Lani</strong>, it was cracked open on the balcony table at 4 pm. With durian's smell, outdoors is the
    right place for it. By late that night the table held mostly peels: rambutan skins, the orange rinds of something
    else, and the empty durian shell.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-durian.webp'} alt="A spiky durian, cracked open to show a pale yellow segment of flesh, on a gray outdoor table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Durian on the balcony, 4 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-night-haul.webp'} alt="Plastic bags of rambutan skins and orange peels on a dark table at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>What was left, 10:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Hilo again, September 2026</h3>
  <p>
    A year later I was back, this time with my wife, and on the second day we crossed the island to Hilo again. By 12:32 on September 8, 2026, the haul
    was lined up along a bench on the bayfront near the Moʻoheau Bandstand: dragon fruit, a pineapple, mangoes,
    papaya, passion fruit, an avocado, two cacao pods, and a large green pear-shaped thing we took for a fruit. It
    turned out to be a kind of squash. Two minutes later one of the cacao pods was split open. Inside, the beans sit
    in a cluster wrapped in white pulp, which is sweet and is what you eat; the beans themselves are what becomes
    chocolate.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-fruit-bay.webp'} alt="A row of tropical fruit in bags on a bench, with a lawn, the bayfront road and Hilo Bay behind: cacao, mangoes, an avocado, a green pear-shaped squash, a pineapple and dragon fruit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hilo bayfront lineup, 12:32 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-cacao.webp'} alt="A cacao pod split open to show white pulp-covered beans, beside a whole yellow pod." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cacao pod, opened, 12:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Our two favorites came next. The papaya cost a dollar and was very sweet, deep orange inside, and the mango was
    sweeter still, eaten straight from the hand with the bay behind it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-papaya.webp'} alt="A papaya broken open in one hand, orange flesh and a cluster of black seeds, with the bayfront behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The one-dollar papaya, 12:41 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-mango.webp'} alt="A half-eaten golden mango held up in one hand against a blurred lawn and the sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The mango, 12:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Before them, a guava turned out pink all the way through, and after them came the pineapple, pale white inside.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-guava.webp'} alt="A guava broken in half in two hands, its flesh bright pink." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A pink guava, 12:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-pineapple.webp'} alt="Two hands holding chunks of pale white pineapple over a bag of cut pieces on the bench." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pineapple, 1:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The one thing missing was durian. We looked for it this time and found none.
  </p>
  <p>
    The next afternoon, at a coffee farm in <strong>Holualoa</strong> above Kona, the fruit was still on the plants.
    Coffee is a fruit before it is a bean: the branches were hung with cherries in every stage, green, yellow and
    red, and a wooden box by the pulper was full of ripe red ones.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-cherries-branch.webp'} alt="Coffee branches over green ground cover, hung with clusters of green, yellow and red coffee cherries." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coffee cherries in Holualoa, 2:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-cherries-box.webp'} alt="A wooden box full of red coffee cherries beside burlap sacks on a green-painted wooden deck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coffee cherries waiting for the pulper.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On the same farm a banana plant had a bunch of small green bananas, with the purple flower still hanging below
    it. The bananas point up toward the leaves, not down.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'banana-bunch.webp'} alt="A bunch of small green bananas on a banana plant, with a purple banana flower hanging below and large leaves around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bananas on the plant, 3:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On Oʻahu later that week, the island tour made a brief stop at the Dole Plantation, in the pineapple country in the
    middle of the island. The whole trip is in <a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">the Hawaiʻi 2026 post</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Kauaʻi, April 2026</h3>
  <p>
    On Kauaʻi in April 2026 the fruit came in a bowl first. On April 24, at the Hanalei Valley lookout, a small stand
    called <strong>Hanalei Organic</strong> sold açaí bowls topped with banana, figs and berries, with the green
    valley below the counter. That afternoon we went east to <strong>Kīlauea</strong>, and by 1:28 a small haul of
    fruit was laid out on a wooden table: a big papaya, mottled green and orange, a small green citrus, two yellow fruits, a brown
    one, and a hand of small green bananas, short and fat, fanned out like a crown. That trip is in <a href="/misc/kauai-2026">the Kauaʻi post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'acai-valley.webp'} alt="An açaí bowl topped with banana, figs and berries and a second bowl on a wooden counter at the edge of an open-air stand, with red ti leaves and a green valley and mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Açaí above Hanalei Valley, 12:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-fruit.webp'} alt="Fruit laid out on a wooden table: a large mottled papaya, a small green citrus, a hand of short green bananas, two yellow fruits and a brown one." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kīlauea haul, 1:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="strawberries">Strawberries</h2>
  <p>
    Strawberries come from everywhere: from supermarkets in plastic clamshells,
    from a farm stand in Irvine, and for one spring from a pot at home. The most unusual ones were white.
  </p>

  <h3>White strawberries in Portland, Maine</h3>
  <p>
    In January 2025 I flew to Boston, where my wife had already been for a week, to bring our dog Wangcai home, and
    on the Saturday we drove up to Portland, Maine. That evening at the hotel there was a clamshell of white strawberries, sold as "Berry de Blanc", and they were photographed from
    every angle. White strawberries are not unripe red ones. They are varieties bred to stay pale, and you can tell
    they are ripe from the faint blush on the skin and the red of the seeds. One berry, shaped like a molar, got a
    photo session of its own after midnight.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'white-strawberries.webp'} alt="A close-up of pale white strawberries with red seeds in a clear plastic box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>White strawberries, Portland, 8:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'white-strawberries-close.webp'} alt="A closer view of white strawberries with pink-tinged seeds in a clear clamshell on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same box, 8:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There was also a box of ordinary red strawberries, with a few of the white ones tucked under it. The rest of that weekend is in <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">Bringing Wangcai Home</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-red-strawberries.webp'} alt="Red strawberries in an open plastic clamshell, with a few white strawberries visible at the bottom edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The red ones, 8:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>At the farm stand</h3>
  <p>
    On April 4, 2025, we stopped at the strawberry stand at <strong>Tanaka Farms</strong> in Irvine. Spring is
    strawberry season in Orange County, and the stand had rows of open cardboard baskets on a wooden table, each
    filled with large berries standing on end.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tanaka-stand.webp'} alt="Rows of cardboard baskets filled with large red strawberries on a wooden table at a farm stand." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tanaka Farms, Irvine, 2:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>From the supermarket</h3>
  <p>
    The rest come from the supermarket. In May 2025 my wife's photo of a box of Driscoll's
    "Sweetest Batch" at home shows the darkest strawberries in this post, almost burgundy.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'driscolls-box.webp'} alt="A clear plastic box of very dark red strawberries with a yellow Driscoll's Sweetest Batch label." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Supermarket strawberries, May 9, 2025.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Grown at home</h3>
  <p>
    And one spring we had our own. In March 2024, when we lived in Los Angeles, there was a strawberry plant in a
    black nursery pot at home, with fresh green leaves. By April 13 it was in a bigger grey pot and had one small
    red strawberry, hanging over the soil. It is the only one in the photos.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strawberry-plant.webp'} alt="A young strawberry plant with bright green leaves in a small black nursery pot on a dark stand." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The plant, March 27, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strawberry-in-pot.webp'} alt="A single small red strawberry hanging over the soil inside a grey pot, under strawberry leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harvest, April 13, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="berries">Other berries, and grapes</h2>
  <p>
    The other berries mostly arrived on top of something. In Cancún in July 2022, breakfast was a bowl of melon,
    pineapple, strawberries, raspberries and blueberries on granola. The açaí bowls in Hawaiʻi came with berries on top as well.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'breakfast-bowl.webp'} alt="A bowl of melon, pineapple, strawberries, raspberries and blueberries on granola." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Breakfast in Cancún, July 2022.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Grapes count as berries too, botanically. We were in Temecula wine country on April 22, 2023, too early in the
    season for any grapes. What the vines had instead, in my wife's photo at 1:26, were the tiny green flower clusters
    that become the bunches by late summer.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'temecula-grape-flowers.webp'} alt="A young grapevine shoot with broad green leaves and a small cluster of green flower buds, against bare soil." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A future bunch of grapes, Temecula, 1:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The oranges at <strong>The Huntington</strong> in San Marino were further along. On March 12, 2023, we walked
    past citrus trees there with the branches full of small orange fruit.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'huntington-oranges.webp'} alt="Dense green leaves of a citrus tree covered in small orange fruit under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Citrus at The Huntington, 4:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="rare">Durian, and other rare finds</h2>
  <p>
    In Los Angeles, the Asian supermarkets of the San Gabriel Valley carry fruit that is hard to find elsewhere. On the morning of March 16, 2023, we spent a while in the produce section of
    <strong>SF Supermarket</strong> in San Gabriel. One table was nothing but cherimoya from Australia, each one on its
    own yellow foam tray, with red dragon fruit at the end. Another was green mangoes, each wrapped in a white foam
    net, next to a crate of small green jujubes.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sfsm-cherimoya.webp'} alt="A supermarket produce table of green, scaly cherimoya on yellow and white foam trays, with red dragon fruit at the right edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cherimoya, 10:17 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sfsm-green-mangoes.webp'} alt="Rows of green mangoes, each in a white foam net and plastic tray, on a black display table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Green mangoes, 10:26 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The durians were in a freezer case, whole, each in a yellow mesh bag, under a sign for frozen durian from
    Vietnam.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sfsm-frozen-durian.webp'} alt="A freezer case full of whole durians, each in a yellow mesh bag." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Whole durians in the freezer, 10:29 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A year later, on March 24, 2024, two whole durians in yellow nets were at home, and by five o'clock one of them
    was open on the kitchen counter, its shell split along a seam to show the pale yellow flesh inside.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'durian-pair-2024.webp'} alt="Two large spiky durians in yellow mesh nets, leaning against a wall on a wooden floor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two durians, March 24, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'durian-home-2024.webp'} alt="A durian with half of its spiky shell removed, showing pale yellow flesh, on a granite counter." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One of them, opened, 5:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The most recent one was on September 25, 2026, at home, a little after eleven at night: a durian split
    into two halves on the counter, with the yellow pods still sitting in the white shell. With the Big Island
    one in 2025, that makes four durians in this post, not counting the freezer case.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'durian-home-2026.webp'} alt="A durian split into two halves on a white tiled counter, showing soft yellow pods in white chambers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Durian at home, 11:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The furthest of the rare finds came from <strong>Sanya</strong>, the city at the southern tip of Hainan, China's
    tropical island, during our trip to China in February 2023. On the evening of February 5 there was a bag of sugar apples: knobbly, pale green fruit
    made of rounded segments, a relative of the cherimoya. The rest of that
    trip is in <a href="/misc/china-2023">the China 2023 post</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sanya-sugar-apples.webp'} alt="Three knobbly pale green sugar apples in a white plastic bag." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sugar apples in Sanya, 6:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    That is the collection so far: markets, benches and farms on trips, and at home the supermarket, a farm stand
    in strawberry season, and once a pot of our own.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
